# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Spanish for the vocabulary that stands beside the lives.

TEXT = {the phrase the index shows: the Spanish}. The keys are the phrases
exactly as the index writes them, so a rendering cannot quietly attach
itself to the wrong saint.

The register was settled before a line of this file was written and is set
down in docs/SPANISH.md. Spanish came to the Saints page with its lives
already written, so the register was read off them rather than decided here.

Spanish belongs with Greek, Romanian and German and not with Russian: san
Nicolas is ordinary Spanish. One distinction is kept throughout, and it is
the one Spanish Orthodox usage keeps - the monastic saint is venerable,
nuestro venerable padre, and never merely santo.

The trap in this language is its own Western church vocabulary, which is at
hand for every word and carries a different Church behind it. Dormicion and
not Asuncion, which is a Roman dogma of 1950. Divina Liturgia and not Misa.
Padre de la Iglesia and not Doctor de la Iglesia, which is a Roman category
with a list. Venerable for the monastic and not beato, which is a degree in
a Roman process; bienaventurado is the word for the fools for Christ.
Reliquias and not restos, traslacion and hallazgo for the two distinct
feasts that invencion blurs.

San, Santo and Santa stand before the name of a thing - a church, a
monastery, a town - and not before a person named as a saint in his own
right, who is named by his rank: el santo martir Jorge, nuestro venerable
padre Sergio, el monasterio de San Sabas.

Slavic names are transliterated the Spanish way and the written accent
carries the Russian stress, because Spanish stress is fixed by the ending
and an unmarked Novgorod is read novgoROD: Novgorod, Vladimir, Suzdal,
Chernigov, Radonezh, Optina, all accented in the file itself.

A language may later declare PARTS and an expand() to assemble the compound
place-names from pieces rendered once, as the older languages do. It is an
optimisation and not a requirement: everything written in TEXT stands over
whatever expand() builds.
"""
TEXT = {
}

